  "spiritual_reading": "# Spiritual Translation\n\n** ¶2:**\n\nAll these aff ections that are perceived in the mind , and also the remaining living states that arise within the natural man , are confirmed in three degrees of formation . Some come into being as incomplete or malformed spiritual begin nings — states that never reach full ness of life . Others, however, develop of themselves according to the form inher ent in their own nature, sh aping themselves from within.\n\n**¶3:**\n\nLikewise , the spiritual sust enance of these affections is of three kinds , according to the measure of wisdom dwelling within them, if they are disposed to receive it. Some take in interior tru ths and the works that proceed from them; they are nourished by interior tru ths and by good works. And they part ake entirely of the three fundamental spiritual forms according to their origin and the condition of their life while they remain in the natural mind. Yet they are held capt ive within their own states . The Divine Mediator alone gov erns all the fac ulties of the mind .\n\n**¶4:**\n\nFirst and most generally , name these: the simpl est natural knowledges and the most rud imentary perceptions. From these were also formed the per ceptions ro oted in falsity and b itterness — hollow appearances , w ounding fals ities, and all states resemb ling them.\n\n**¶5 :** For that intention belongs to the ruling loves of self, which dev our living affections. Yet the simple natural kn owledges and innocent natural goods were planted as they are in their own essence. Nevertheless, the intention that descended from the ruling loves — ins ofar as it first manif ests in works and natural knowledges and is not yet impl anted into the corru pted will and understanding — formed those per ceptions that yield genu inely n ourishing good works , whose natural goods are in wardly sweet.\n\n**¶ 6:** Again, the intention that descended from the dominant powers that consume both living affections * and* natural knowledges and simple goods — this has formed per ceptions that produce nothing of spiritual n ourishment. Their works belong neither to what is good nor to what is evil — they are spiritu ally bar ren, proce eding from a divided will that can commit itself to nothing.",
  "reconstructions": [
    {
      "paragraph": 2,
      "reconstructed_text": "\"All these animals that you see, and also the rest of the [crea]tures [that walk upon the face] of the earth, confirm them in three likeness[es of formation]: some from [the rulers'] abortions. Others, however, form their own selves in their shape [and their likeness from within].\""
    },
    {
      "paragraph": 3,
      "reconstructed_text": "\"Also, their nourishment is thre[efold, according to the] light in them, if they shall wish [to receive it]. [Some among them,] they eat see[ds and] fr[uit of the trees]; they eat seeds and fruit. The[y receive] it entirely, and they [bear] the three archetyp[es] from their nature and the [condition of] the time when they are on the ea[rth in this world]; they are seized [and held captive] in their [own natures]. The Ambassador [alone governs] all powers [of the soul].\""
    },
    {
      "paragraph": 4,
      "reconstructed_text": "\"[Now] it [shall be named], first [of all]. Plant them generally to this name: shoots and grass. It formed the bad, bitter trees, the reeds and the thorns and the [thistles] and these others that are like them.\""
    },
    {
      "paragraph": 5,
      "reconstructed_text": "\"For that thought belongs to the flesh-eating rulers. Still, these [that were] planted (belong to) grass and gourds, since they are in their essence. Nevertheless, [this t]ho[ugh]t that came down from the rulers, as they are in fruit and grass first and shall not be planted in flesh and blood, it formed the trees that shall give nourishing fru[it], as their gourds are sweet.\""
    },
    {
      "paragraph": 6,
      "reconstructed_text": "\"Again, this thou[ght] that came down from the powers that eat m[ea]t and also eat grass and gourds, it has formed the trees that shall not produce any nourishment. Their fruits belong neither to good nor evil.\""
    }
